  "narration": [
    {
      "t": 0.5,
      "text": "Two layers, both setting the same thing. Which one wins is the opposite of what most people guess."
    },
    {
      "t": 4.8,
      "text": "One layer says three."
    },
    {
      "t": 9.8,
      "text": "The other says nine. Both under the same name."
    },
    {
      "t": 14.8,
      "text": "We put this one into the order first. Note the position it reports."
    },
    {
      "t": 19.8,
      "text": "And the other one after it."
    },
    {
      "t": 24.8,
      "text": "Now merge the stack into a file."
    },
    {
      "t": 29.8,
      "text": "Three won. That is the layer we stacked first, not the last one."
    },
    {
      "t": 34.8,
      "text": "So if we want the other one to win, we take this one out of the order..."
    },
    {
      "t": 39.8,
      "text": "...and add it back, which puts it at the end."
    },
    {
      "t": 44.8,
      "text": "The order now reads top to bottom, and the top one is the winner."
    },
    {
      "t": 49.8,
      "text": "Merge it again into a second file."
    },
    {
      "t": 54.8,
      "text": "Nine this time. Same two layers, different order, different answer."
    },
    {
      "t": 58.5,
      "text": "The one you stack first is the one that wins. Reorder the stack, not the layers."
    }
  ],
  "notes": "Teaches first-stacked-wins precedence by merging the same two layers in both orders and showing both results."
}
